WEBVTT

NOTE English visual guide. Editorial descriptions of on-screen concepts; no spoken narration.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.200
A Transformer block is a repeatable unit combining attention with a feed-forward network.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Attention mixes information across positions, giving each position a context-dependent

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.000
representation.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.400
A residual connection adds a sublayer’s output to its input, preserving a direct path through the

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.800
block.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.800
Layer normalization computes statistics over the feature coordinates of each token independently.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.600
For the example [2, −1, 4, 3], mean is 2 and variance is 3.5. Standardization is followed by a

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:57.200
learned scale and bias.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.200
The feed-forward network transforms each token separately. This example expands width 64 to 256,

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:08.400
applies GELU and projects back to 64.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.200
The second residual connection adds the feed-forward result back to the residual stream.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.200
Pre-norm places normalization before the sublayer; post-norm places it after the residual addition.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.600
Small training comparisons illustrate how normalization placement and warmup can affect

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:43.200
optimization. Their outcomes depend on experimental conditions.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.200
Stacking blocks lets later layers build on contextual features produced by earlier layers.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.200
Pause and distinguish operations that mix positions from those that mix feature coordinates within a

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.400
position.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.200
Recap: attention, residual paths, normalization and the feed-forward network work together. The next

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:20.400
lesson assembles complete blocks into a language model.
